  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 152 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Little in the manner of impactful weather is expected \nover the next several days. High pressure will move offshore, and \nthat will allow warm air to gradually filter into the region under \nsteady south to southwest flow. This will bring 50s today, and then \n60s tomorrow. It'll still be on the dry side, but winds will \ngenerally be lighter where the lowest humidity values will be. \nNighttime temperatures will also trend warmer, with only cooler \nhollows of sheltered valleys east of any mountain slopes below \nfreezing tonight. There's still a tilted mid to upper level ridge \nthat will be positioned to our west through about Tuesday. As it \nbegins to translate eastwards, depending on the positioning of an \nupper vort, the surge of warm, moist advection could become \ncollocated with marginal synoptic forcing. This would result in an \narea of clouds and light rain, mainly along the international border \nTuesday into Tuesday night. Not every model scenario depicts rain, \nand so PoPs are mainly at or below 50 percent at this time.\n\nOnce the upper ridge is offshore, temperatures will climb into the \nmid 60s to lower 70s for Wednesday in response to somewhat tighter \npressure gradients as a surface low tracks over far northern Quebec \nProvince. A few daily record highs may be in striking distance. \nUshering in the warmth will be south winds gusting around 15-25 mph \nwith localized observations around 30 mph, especially in the broader \nvalleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Model consensus favors a full meridional trough ejecting \nout of the Plains Wednesday night with a cold front passing \nthrough the Northeast between Thursday and Friday. Timing spread\nhas narrowed a little, but model discrepancies remain on how \nfast the high pressure shifts eastward, the timing of the front \nitself, and the strength of frontal dynamics. Some models are \nstalling the boundary in the vicinity of Vermont streaming ample\nmoisture across the region while others are more progressive \nwith flow resulting in more distinct rounds of rainfall. While \nthe details are sparse, most guidance agrees that there is a \ngood shot at an extended period of rain chances with the \ntrailing upper low becoming at least partially cut off. This \nwould promote lingering shower chances through the weekend \npotentially into early next week. The details that are more \npredictable are continued warm temperatures under southerly flow\nThursday with some southerly breezes ongoing as well. Beyond \nThursday, temperatures are favored to cool back towards seasonal\naverages with coldest air locked well north of the region.",
